re carbon-based materials  they have a fairly good nutrient holding  capacity as well or cation exchange  capacity is what we talk about  and then we also have inert components  that are not carbon based so those would  be like this this picture on the bottom  right which is stone wall or rock wool  is a spun insulation material  it has very good physical properties  it's good at holding on to water and uh  and  allowing air space but it doesn't have  an additional nutrient holding capacity  so when we're when we're growing plants  in this type of substrate we have to  supply  nutrients every time that we're watering  perlite and vermiculite are other  examples of that as well as lightweight  expanded clay aggregate  so it's important to know the materials  that you're using  you can talk with the manufacturer of  them  to see if they they should do their own  in-house testing for these physical and  chemical properties that i'm about to  talk to  that would be a sign of a reputable  supplier  if they can't provide you with a  nutritional test or information on the  physical properties  you can also  do some of the testing yourself which  i'll talk about and other components you  can you can send to a lab  um so when we're talking about the  physical properties this is the balance  of the  water holding  and  air porosity um and so we when we fill a  container with a mix we have the  substrate itself for the particles  itself  and then we have  space that's not taken up by those  particles and when we water a container  to container capacity that is watering a  complete container fully with water and  allowing it to drip drain  then of those total pore spaces they're  going to be divided into some of those  spaces are still holding air so that's  called the air filled porosity and  that's a percent  by volume and then we have water holding  capacity so those are the pore spaces  that are filled  by water  and so the right soilless media is going  to have a balance between the moisture  holding and the air porosity  and one of the ways that we can think  about that is that water holding  capacity and aeration balance  and the height of the water table in the  pot  is  really only affected by that particle  size where a smaller particle size is  going to be like that container on the  right that holds more water and less air  and if you have a larger particle size  you're going to hold more air and less  water and  often you will see that potting mixes  are formulated to contain  multiple components  so we might have like peat  as well as perlite and vermiculite  because together they they give us the  physical properties that we need their  combined  particle size  balances moisture and air  so the metric that we're really looking  for here  is that the total porosity should be  equal to 50 percent by volume  and then the water holding capacity  within that should be at least 40  percent  and then that means the air porosity  should be at least 10 percent because  air porosity plus water holding equals  total porosity  and where that 10 comes from is in the  traditional greenhouse world not  necessarily the cannabis world but they  found below that 10  there's poorer root growth and not as  enough not enough oxygen for for to  support a really um good robust root  system and where that 40 comes from is  if that the lower that number is the  more frequently you need to irrigate  your container so if you were overhead  watering maybe you would have to water  three times a day if you had really poor  water holding capacity  and just an example of physical  properties of some of the different  sub substrate components that we use  often peat by itself p peat comes in  different layers depending on  how long it's been degrading  and some of the kind of virgin peat  that's toward the top that has been  degraded for a less period of time  actually could serve as a potting mix by  itself um it has sufficient  water holding and it has at least 10  airfield porosity  more often than not we have to add  perlite or vermiculite to peat to  improve its airfield porosity  and then coconut core um is often used  as a material by itself coconut core  also varies in its  how well degraded it is or how how  fibrous it is  but you can find many coconut cores that  by themselves have sufficient air  porosity  um have very good water holding capacity  and interesting in this case it's 95  total porosity so that means when you  buy that coconut coir  you're only getting 5 by volume is  actually that fiber that you're paying  money for and the rest is  is air space or water space  so i'm going to move on to chemical  properties  so ph  really affects the availability of  nutrients in the root zone and it's been  said that about half of the nutrient  disorders that we have um are related to  an improper ph and not due to lack of  the nutrient itself  so this graph shows us availability of  the different nutrients that are  important to the plant as the ph  increases from  four to eight and so with some of the  nutrients like nitrogen and potassium  we're pretty lucky they're actually  widely available  in the root zone across that broad ph  range  and then we have several nutrients like  phosphorus and then all of the  micronutrients iron manganese boron and  so on we're really above a ph of about  six and a half in a soilless media um  those get locked up and are not  available for roots to take up  and one thing about soilless media is  they do have less buffering capacity  than our traditional field soils so with  the field soil we could get away with a  higher ph but in a soilless potting mix  we're really looking for this more  narrow range  on the low end of the spectrum if we  move below a ph of about five and a half  we have issues with calcium and  magnesium availability  and at low ph we can also get too high  of phosphorus solubility that means our  phosphorus can leach out of the  container  too readily  so really the optimal ph we're looking  for for for hemp or cannabis in general  is five and a half to  six and a half um and for monitoring we  we recommend targeting 5.8 to 6.2  um and then if you're say tracking ph uh  every week which i'll talk about  once you track out of that range you  would start taking action so that you  have some time before you get out of  that five and a half six and a half  range  um so there's several factors that  affect root zone ph  and one is the your water source itself  many of our well water sources in new  york are high in their alkalinity  there's limestone deposits um under the  earth um and so the carbonates and  bicarbonates in that water  are like liquid limestone they  essentially increase the ph of our root  zone over time  and so we often see that in western new  york and upstate new york  where our well water is high in  alkalinity and our ph is creeping up  over time  the fertilizers that we use also affect  the ph so a more ammonium or urea based  fertilizer is going to decrease the ph  and a nitrate-based fertilizer increases  the ph so that's actually a tool that we  have to use to adjust ph is adjusting  our fertilizer  and then we can also use acids to to  decrease ph  so i mentioned problems with ph being  too low  which  especially our deficiency of calcium and  magnesium  leaching a phosphorus and if we get very  low sometimes less than like four and a  half or five um  iron and manganese and zinc and copper  become very available to the roots to  take up and we can in some cases see  toxicity of those micronutrients  and then when ph is too high which tends  to be a uh more common problem with the  waters that we have  in uh in upstate new york uh we get  deficiency of those micronutrients iron  manganese and so on and so um  uh this is a photo of iron deficiency  which is uh which affects the upper  leaves the youngest leaves on the plant  um and we get yellowing of those upper  leaves um starting at the base where  they attach to the stem um and then in  particular the veins themselves will  stay green but the intervenal region  will be lighter yellow  the other major chemical property that i  want to talk about is electrical  conductivity  or ec and we use this as an overall  measure of fertility  what it does is it allows us to use a  handheld sensor  to get a measure of the overall salts  that are dissolved in water and it takes  advantage of the property of water that  pure water doesn't conduct electricity  but water with dissolved ions in it  can conduct more electricity  so low ec indicates low overall salts or  low overall fertility and then a high ec  indicates high fertility or overall high  salts  with with hemp and i'll talk about  testing methods but with with the pour  through testing method we're looking for  an ec reading of about 1 to 2.5  depending on the the crop stage  and this is a photo of nitrogen  deficiency which we would get often with  overall low fertility or low ec  which is lower leaf yellowing and small  plant size  um so we're still in our early days of  of knowing exact fertilizer  recommendations for cannabis that's  actually or hemp that's actually one of  the things that i study  but there's there's recommendations  where  we adjust the fertilizer rate often we  talk about the parts per million  nitrogen and then the other nutrients  are adjusted in proportion to that uh  and so at the at the early vegetative  stage um during propagation say we're at  100 to 125 parts per million nitrogen  um during late flowering and or late  vegetative and into peak flowering we'll  be up to about 200 parts per million  nitrogen  and then we'll cut  back down on that about two weeks  before  harvest  so that gives us a target ec of 1 to 1.5  up to 2 to 2.5 during the period when  the plant is using the most fertilizer  um so i want to mention briefly some of  the in-house methods that we have so if  you if you  buy a ph and ec meter for about 200  total um you could do either the pour  through or the one to two dilution  method uh those are those are easy to do  in-house and then laboratories will  usually use the saturated media extract  method  which is a different method  so you can buy handheld ph and ec pens  they'll they'll cost about a hundred  dollars per meter  you want to use a reputable brand some  that i've had good luck with not to  exclude others but are blue lab hanna  instruments and oakton  some are dual purpose so they can  measure both ph and ec  be sure to check the ec unit there are  some different units that are used i  like the ones that measure ec and in um  decisions per meter  instead of ppm  and then the ph meter needs to be  calibrated every day that you use it so  you get a ph4 and a ph 7 reference  solution and it'll take about a minute  to calibrate it before you use it  so for the pour through method which is  the the kind of easiest non-destructive  method you're going to water your plants  about an hour before taking the  measurement so that they can get to  container capacity  um you're then going to place a plastic  saucer under the container  and then depending on the size of the  container you're going to pour a known  amount of  um distilled water on top  and then that's going to push out a  leachate sample so with a six inch pot  we're using about 50 milliliters of  solution  and then you collect that leachate  sample and then that's what you're  putting your your ph and your ec pens  into  and analyzing  with the one to two dilution method or  if you send a sample to a lab for the  saturated media extract method you're  taking slices or core of the media from  where the roots are actively growing so  we find that salts can build up at the  top surface  as well as the bottom and so we want to  take  samples of soil from the middle of the  container where the roots are actively  growing  you would take samples from 10  representative plants pool those samples  together  if you're going to send in a sample to a  lab talk to the lab but they'll usually  require one to two cups of material  and then if you're doing an in-house  test for using the one to two dilution  method um what you're gonna do is um air  dry that sample overnight um compare it  uh combine one part by volume of the  media so often i like using little dixie  cups so three ounce cups so i'll take a  three ounce scoop of that and then two  cups of distilled water  mix thoroughly and let that stand for 30  minutes  and then you can use your ph and ec  meter  to test that water extract  this is also a really good method to use  when testing a new potting mix so if you  don't have the ph and ec information  from the manufacturer you can test a new  potting mix to make sure it's not too  high in salts like some of our compost  based mixes  and it can also tell you about the the  overall fertility  the saturated media extract would be a  method that the laboratory uses um and i  won't go into that that too much but  then typically besides testing ph and ec  they're going to test the individual  nutrients the n p and k and so on  and so it's nice to know what your mix  is at the beginning  and then if you have questions about  fertility over time you can you can send  a media sample into the lab  so this in-house monitoring for ph and  ec really  is most effective if you can do it  weekly  or at least every two weeks and then  graph that over time and track that over  time so here's an example i had plants  growing in containers with a peat  perlite potting mix  we would sample them every seven days or  so  we started taking action when we got  above a ph of six and a half so we  switched to a more acidic fertilizer for  a week  we did that at day 28 and it was  effective  we started getting out of range again  about 20 days later and we switched to  an acidic fertilizer again for for  another week  so overall methodology for correcting  for ph um if your ph is testing high um  the quick method that you have is to do  a one-time drench with acids um either  two ounces of sulfuric acid or three and  a half ounces of phosphoric acid  per 100 gallons of water a lot of  growers will use that through a  fertilizer injector that's say a one to  one hundred injector so then their stock  solution would be two ounces of sulfuric  per one gallon of stock solution and  then that gets diluted down to the  injector to 100 gallons of water gradual  methods to correct for a high ph are to  switch to a more ammonium-based  fertilizer  or if you know that your tap water  source is high in alkalinity is to  continually add  a dilute amount of acid to decrease your  water alkalinity down to 80 parts per  million calcium carbonate  correcting for low ph um which is less  of an issue here i see it more on like  long island for example where they have  very pure water we can use flowable lime  or potassium bicarbonate  you do want to follow very carefully the  label instructions because those  the flowable lime if it's in contact  with the foliage can cause a burn to the  leaf and so you have to go back  afterwards and mist  any material off of leaves if it got on  them and you also want to flush your  your irrigation lines after using those  materials  a more gradual method that we have is to  switch to a more nitrate nitrate-based  fertilizer  and then ec correction so if we have an  eye a high ec  um  that means that we're either over  fertilizing or  our water source or substrate is high in  salts so a quick method is to leach  thoroughly with tap water  and  leech so that we get about a 50 leaching  fraction so about half of the water that  you add to each pot is leaching out of  the bottom  and do that twice and that's going to  reduce the ec by about half  the longer term control would be to  reduce your fertilizer concentration  leech  more frequently with a 20 leaching  fraction so just do that periodically  and then test your water source to see  if it's high in salts or test your  substrate source to see if they're high  in salts to try to identify the cause  correcting a low ec essentially is an  indication that your fertilizer strength  is not high enough so you need to  increase your fertilizer strength or  reduce your leaching fraction if you are  leaching at each irrigation event  with that i just wanted to point out a  few of the commercial testing labs these  will all do  tissue substrate and water analysis the  cost tends to vary from about thirty  dollars um to forty five dollars um and  i find that growers typically once they  find a lab that they that works well for  them  they tend to stick with that lab because  every lab's results will be a little bit  different just kind of funny but  they like if you want to do an apples to  apples comparison you typically stick  with a given lab after a certain point  in time  some of these labs for example i know  for sure that jr peters and macro micro  laboratory  will do  tissue hemp analysis and they have um  sufficiency ranges that that the  laboratory can give you for  what percent nitrogen should be in the  leaves for example based on the stage  that you're at  all right with that i will end and we'll  see if larry gives us any time for  questions  yeah thanks very much neil unfortunately  we're going to try to get back on  schedule  and uh in particular neil did not talk  about all of his expertise in lighting  uh so if you have questions about  lighting also uh catch up with neil he  is an encyclopedia of knowledge so  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

