g high um  the quick method that you have is to do  a one-time drench with acids um either  two ounces of sulfuric acid or three and  a half ounces of phosphoric acid  per 100 gallons of water a lot of  growers will use that through a  fertilizer injector that's say a one to  one hundred injector so then their stock  solution would be two ounces of sulfuric  per one gallon of stock solution and  then that gets diluted down to the  injector to 100 gallons of water gradual  methods to correct for a high ph are to  switch to a more ammonium-based  fertilizer  or if you know that your tap water  source is high in alkalinity is to  continually add  a dilute amount of acid to decrease your  water alkalinity down to 80 parts per  million calcium carbonate  correcting for low ph um which is less  of an issue here i see it more on like  long island for example where they have  very pure water we can use flowable lime  or potassium bicarbonate  you do want to follow very carefully the  label instructions because those  the flowable lime if it's in contact  with the foliage can cause a burn to the  leaf and so you have to go back  afterwards and mist  any material off of leaves if it got on  them and you also want to flush your  your irrigation lines after using those  materials  a more gradual method that we have is to  switch to a more nitrate nitrate-based  fertilizer  and then ec correction so if we have an  eye a high ec  um  that means that we're either over  fertilizing or  our water source or substrate is high in  salts so a quick method is to leach  thoroughly with tap water  and  leech so that we get about a 50 leaching  fraction so about half of the water that  you add to each pot is leaching out of  the bottom  and do that twice and that's going to  reduce the ec by about half  the longer term control would be to  reduce your fertilizer concentration  leech  more frequently with a 20 leaching  fraction so just do that periodically  and then test your water source t